#!/bin/sh
# opencode — kit-installed wrapper that SHADOWS the real `opencode` binary.
#
# The opencode base image's entrypoint runs the bare, unqualified command
# `opencode`. This wrapper is dropped at ~/.local/bin/opencode, which is FIRST on
# the image PATH (ahead of the npm-global bin where the real opencode lives), so
# the sandbox's interactive `acq run` startup runs THIS instead.
#
# WHAT THIS WRAPPER IS FOR (Paseo kit): the Paseo daemon + web UI are started and
# supervised by the kit's STARTUP script (paseo-start.sh) on every sandbox start —
# so the UI comes up on its own, with or without this wrapper. This wrapper does
# two kit-specific jobs on the interactive `acq run` path, then keeps the sandbox
# alive:
#
#   1. PIN PASEO'S WORKTREE ROOT to the current project. Paseo keeps agent git
#      worktrees under a SINGLE GLOBAL root; there is no per-project option, and a
#      relative root resolves against PASEO_HOME. On `acq run <path>` THIS wrapper
#      is the entrypoint and runs with cwd = the primary (first mounted) workspace,
#      so it computes <PWD>/.paseo-worktrees and writes it to
#      $PASEO_HOME/config.json (via paseo-set-worktrees-root.mjs). The daemon reads
#      worktrees.root ONLY at boot (no SIGHUP / no runtime reread), so when the
#      value CHANGES the wrapper bounces the daemon — it kills the supervised
#      daemon child (never the supervisor), and the startup script's
#      supervisor:paseo-daemon loop respawns it, re-reading the new root.
#
#   2. PRINT HOST-CONNECT INSTRUCTIONS and, when it IS PID 1 (the `acq run`
#      entrypoint), HOLD the foreground so that session's sandbox — and thus the
#      daemon/UI — stays up.
#
# AGENT-GENERIC: aside from resolving the real `opencode` for passthrough, this
# wrapper says nothing opencode-specific. There is NO shared live session between
# a terminal TUI and Paseo (Paseo launches its own agent CLIs as children), so the
# wrapper offers no "attach" — it only pins worktrees, reports, and holds PID 1.
# That keeps the kit reusable for other agents: the same file could shadow a
# different agent binary with only the passthrough target changed.
#
# Behavior:
#   opencode                 → (no args) pin the worktree root, print connect info,
#                              then:
#                                • PID 1 (the `acq run` path): we ARE the sandbox
#                                  entrypoint, so we must NOT return — hold forever
#                                  (hold_pid1) so the terminal/session stays open.
#                                • NOT PID 1 (a shell / `acq exec`, with a detached
#                                  `acq create` keeping the sandbox alive): nothing
#                                  to hold — EXIT CLEANLY and return the terminal.
#   opencode <anything...>   → passthrough: exec the REAL opencode with the args
#                              unchanged (run, auth, etc.).
#
# WHY THE WRAPPER MUST NOT RETURN WHEN IT IS PID 1 (keep the sandbox alive):
#   On the interactive `acq run` path this wrapper IS the sandbox entrypoint (it
#   shadows the base image's bare `opencode` CMD via PATH). A Docker sandbox is
#   "running" only while its entrypoint / PID 1 is alive. If this wrapper returns
#   while it is PID 1, PID 1 exits and the sandbox transitions running -> stopped
#   (taking the Paseo daemon/UI with it). So when `$$` is 1 the no-arg path ends by
#   BLOCKING FOREVER in hold_pid1. On a detached `acq create` PID 1 is instead a
#   tini keepalive shim and this wrapper is NOT PID 1 (it is run from a shell /
#   `acq exec`), so it exits cleanly.
#
# The Paseo daemon is UNSECURED (no PASEO_PASSWORD): the sandbox — an ephemeral
# container with proxied, allow-listed egress and no host FS — is the security
# boundary, and its published port is host loopback only. See the README
# "Security note".

set -eu

# Daemon bind. Keep in sync with the kit spec's PASEO_LISTEN / publishedPorts.
PASEO_PORT="$(printf '%s' "${PASEO_LISTEN:-0.0.0.0:6767}" | sed -n 's/.*:\([0-9][0-9]*\)$/\1/p')"
[ -n "$PASEO_PORT" ] || PASEO_PORT=6767

HOME_DIR="${HOME:-/home/agent}"
PASEO_HOME_DIR="${PASEO_HOME:-$HOME_DIR/.paseo}"
STATE_DIR="${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME_DIR/.local/state}/paseo"
DAEMON_LOG="$STATE_DIR/paseo-daemon.log"
SET_ROOT_SCRIPT="$HOME_DIR/paseo-set-worktrees-root.mjs"

# --- Resolve the REAL opencode (never ourselves). ---------------------------
# We are ~/.local/bin/opencode and are first on PATH, so `command -v opencode`
# would find US. Search PATH entries explicitly and skip our own path.
self_dir="$HOME_DIR/.local/bin"
canon() { ( cd "$(dirname "$1")" 2>/dev/null && printf '%s/%s\n' "$(pwd -P)" "$(basename "$1")" ); }
self_canon="$(canon "$self_dir/opencode")"
real_opencode=""
_ifs="$IFS"; IFS=:
for _dir in $PATH; do
  [ -n "$_dir" ] || continue
  _cand="$_dir/opencode"
  { [ -x "$_cand" ] && [ ! -d "$_cand" ]; } || continue
  [ "$(canon "$_cand")" = "$self_canon" ] && continue
  real_opencode="$_cand"
  break
done
IFS="$_ifs"

# --- Passthrough: any arguments go straight to the real opencode. ------------
# (If the real opencode is missing, only fail on the passthrough path — the
# no-arg Paseo path below does not need opencode at all.)
if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then
  if [ -z "$real_opencode" ]; then
    echo "opencode wrapper: could not locate the real opencode binary on PATH" >&2
    exit 127
  fi
  exec "$real_opencode" "$@"
fi

# --- No args: pin the worktree root, report, hold PID 1 if we are entrypoint. -

# 1. Pin Paseo's worktree root to <PWD>/.paseo-worktrees. PWD here is the primary
#    (first mounted) workspace on the `acq run` path. Use an ABSOLUTE path (Paseo
#    resolves a relative root against PASEO_HOME, not the project).
WORKTREES_ROOT="$(pwd -P)/.paseo-worktrees"
worktrees_changed=""
if [ -f "$SET_ROOT_SCRIPT" ] && command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # The helper prints "changed" / "unchanged" on success, or "error: ..." + rc=1.
  _set_out="$(PASEO_HOME="$PASEO_HOME_DIR" node "$SET_ROOT_SCRIPT" --root "$WORKTREES_ROOT" 2>&1 || true)"
  case "$_set_out" in
    changed)   worktrees_changed=yes ;;
    unchanged) : ;;
    *)         echo "opencode: could not pin Paseo worktree root: $_set_out" >&2 ;;
  esac
fi

# 2. If the root changed, bounce the daemon so it re-reads config.json. We kill
#    ONLY the daemon process tree, never the startup script's supervisor loop, so
#    the supervisor relaunches the daemon with the new worktrees.root. The
#    supervisor loop's argv carries the marker "supervisor:paseo-daemon"; the
#    actual daemon is a `paseo daemon start --foreground ...` CHILD of that loop.
#    We select PIDs whose argv matches the daemon-start pattern but EXCLUDE the
#    supervisor marker, and kill just those.
daemon_up() {
  # /api/health is unauthenticated and returns 200 when the daemon is listening.
  curl -fsS "http://127.0.0.1:$PASEO_PORT/api/health" >/dev/null 2>&1
}
if [ -n "$worktrees_changed" ]; then
  echo "opencode: pinned Paseo worktree root to $WORKTREES_ROOT; restarting the daemon to apply it ..."
  for _pid in $(pgrep -u "$(id -u)" -f "paseo daemon start" 2>/dev/null); do
    # Skip the supervisor loop (its cmdline carries the marker).
    if tr '\0' ' ' < "/proc/$_pid/cmdline" 2>/dev/null | grep -q "supervisor:paseo-daemon"; then
      continue
    fi
    kill "$_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  done
  # Wait for the supervisor to bring it back on the new root (bounded).
  _i=0
  while [ "$_i" -lt 30 ]; do
    daemon_up && break
    _i=$((_i + 1))
    sleep 1
  done
fi

# 3. Report status + how to reach it. Give the daemon a brief moment if it is
#    still coming up (first boot installs the CLI first).
if daemon_up; then
  echo "opencode: Paseo daemon + web UI are up on 0.0.0.0:$PASEO_PORT (started by the kit startup script)"
else
  _i=0
  while [ "$_i" -lt 15 ]; do
    daemon_up && break
    _i=$((_i + 1))
    sleep 1
  done
  if daemon_up; then
    echo "opencode: Paseo daemon + web UI are up on 0.0.0.0:$PASEO_PORT"
  else
    echo "opencode: Paseo daemon not answering yet on :$PASEO_PORT; the startup" >&2
    echo "          supervisor keeps retrying — see $DAEMON_LOG" >&2
  fi
fi

cat <<EOF

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Connect from your HOST
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
This sandbox publishes container port $PASEO_PORT (the Paseo daemon API,
WebSocket, and bundled web UI — all on one port) to a host loopback port. Find
the mapped host port from your host shell:

    acq ports <sandbox>        # (sbx ports <sandbox>)

  • Paseo web UI → container $PASEO_PORT → open http://127.0.0.1:<host-port>

Paseo launches its own agent CLIs (opencode among them); there is no shared
live session with a terminal TUI, so drive agents from the browser UI.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
EOF

# Are WE PID 1? This discriminates the two no-arg situations:
#   - PID 1 (we ARE the sandbox entrypoint, i.e. the `acq run` path): we MUST NOT
#     return, or PID 1 exits and the sandbox stops. Hold forever (hold_pid1).
#   - NOT PID 1 (a detached `acq create` is already keeping the sandbox alive via
#     its tini keepalive shim, and we were run from a shell / `acq exec`): the
#     daemon + UI are already supervised by the startup script, so there is
#     nothing for us to hold — EXIT CLEANLY and give the terminal back.
is_pid1() { [ "$$" -eq 1 ]; }

if is_pid1; then
  cat <<EOF

NOTE: you started this with \`acq run\`, so THIS terminal is the sandbox's main
process. It will stay held open — keep this terminal open while you use Paseo
from your browser. Closing it stops the sandbox.

If you did NOT want a held terminal: quit this (Ctrl-C), then bring the sandbox
up detached instead — it keeps running on its own with no terminal attached:
    acq create --name <sandbox> opencode <path>
    acq ports <sandbox>        # find the host port, then open the browser

Daemon log: $DAEMON_LOG
EOF

  hold_pid1() {
    # Block forever without spinning. Prefer an inert sleep loop; never return.
    while :; do
      sleep 86400
    done
  }

  hold_pid1
else
  cat <<EOF

The sandbox keeps running on its own; the Paseo daemon and web UI are supervised
by the kit startup script. This command is exiting and returning your terminal.
Reach Paseo from the host with:
    acq ports <sandbox>        # then open http://127.0.0.1:<host-port-for-$PASEO_PORT>

Daemon log: $DAEMON_LOG
EOF
  exit 0
fi
